{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01711JaXrAN6xERsSL18kb1N","type":"tool_result","content":"296\t// Raw memory access helpers.\n297\t//\n298\t// `perform_computation_with_history` is the only function that dereferences\n299\t// pointers supplied by the caller, and the C code validates none of them: a\n300\t// NULL `history`/`history_count` simply faults (SIGSEGV), a misaligned\n301\t// `*history` is handled by the hardware, and `(*history)[*history_count]`\n302\t// performs plain wrapping address arithmetic for any index.\n303\t//\n304\t// A plain `*ptr` deref in Rust carries compiler-inserted UB checks in debug\n305\t// builds, which turn those faults into `abort()` (SIGABRT) and turn an\n306\t// unaligned access into a panic -- i.e. *different observable behaviour* from\n307\t// the reference C library for exactly the inputs Phase C exercises. Routing\n308\t// every caller-pointer access through byte-sized volatile accesses (alignment 1,\n309\t// so no check can fire) keeps behaviour identical to the C in every build\n310\t// profile: valid pointers produce exactly the same bytes, invalid ones fault\n311\t// exactly the same way.\n312\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n313\t\n314\t/// `*src` — read `T` from a caller-supplied pointer without any inserted check.\n315\t#[inline(always)]\n316\tunsafe fn raw_load<T: Copy>(src: *const T) -> T {\n317\t    let mut out = core::mem::MaybeUninit::<T>::uninit();\n318\t    let dst = out.as_mut_ptr() as *mut u8;\n319\t    let src = src as *const u8;\n320\t    let mut i = 0usize;\n321\t    while i < size_of::<T>() {\n322\t        ptr::write_volatile(dst.add(i), ptr::read_volatile(src.wrapping_add(i)));\n323\t        i += 1;\n324\t    }\n325\t    out.assume_init()\n326\t}\n327\t\n328\t/// `*dst = val` — store `T` through a caller-supplied pointer without any\n329\t/// inserted check.\n330\t#[inline(always)]\n331\tunsafe fn raw_store<T: Copy>(dst: *mut T, val: T) {\n332\t    let src = &val as *const T as *const u8;\n333\t    let dst = dst as *mut u8;\n334\t    let mut i = 0usize;\n335\t    while i < size_of::<T>() {\n336\t        ptr::write_volatile(dst.wrapping_add(i), ptr::read_volatile(src.add(i)));\n337\t        i += 1;\n338\t    }\n339\t}\n340\t\n341\t/// `&(*history)[index]` — C's array subscript is wrapping pointer arithmetic and\n342\t/// is *not* bounds checked, so neither is this (the C code reaches here with\n343\t/// `index` in `..10`, which includes negative values).\n344\t#[inline(always)]\n345\tfn slot(base: *mut ComputationResult, index: c_int) -> *mut ComputationResult {\n346\t    base.wrapping_offset(index as isize)\n347\t}\n348\t\n349\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n350\t// int perform_computation_with_history(int a, int b, Operation op,\n351\t//                                      ComputationResult** history,\n352\t//                                      int* history_count)\n353\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n354\t\n355\t/// ```c\n356\t/// int perform_computation_with_history(int a, int b, Operation op,\n357\t///                                      ComputationResult** history,\n358\t///                                      int* history_count) {\n359\t///     MathOperation math_func = select_operation(op);\n360\t///     int result = math_func(a, b, 0);\n361\t///     if (*history == NULL) {\n362\t///         *history = allocate_results(10);\n363\t///         *history_count = 0;\n364\t///     }\n365\t///     if (*history_count < 10) {\n366\t///         (*history)[*history_count].value = result;\n367\t///         (*history)[*history_count].timestamp = get_computation_timestamp();\n368\t///         (*history)[*history_count].status = STATUS_SUCCESS;\n369\t///         (*history_count)++;\n370\t///     }\n371\t///     return result;\n372\t/// }\n373\t/// ```\n374\t///\n375\t/// The order of the two `if`s, the field assignment order and the timestamp\n376\t/// call site are all preserved. Note that the C code dereferences `*history`\n377\t/// without re-checking it after `allocate_results`, so a failed allocation\n378\t/// faults; that behaviour is reproduced rather than fixed.\n379\t///\n380\t/// # Safety\n381\t/// `history` and `history_count` must be valid, writable pointers, and\n382\t/// `*history` must be either NULL or an array of at least 10\n383\t/// `ComputationResult`s.\n384\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n385\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn perform_computation_with_history(\n386\t    a: c_int,\n387\t    b: c_int,\n388\t    op: Operation,\n389\t    history: *mut *mut ComputationResult,\n390\t    history_count: *mut c_int,\n391\t) -> c_int {\n392\t    let math_func = select_operation(op);\n393\t\n394\t    let result = math_func(a, b, 0);\n395\t\n396\t    // if (*history == NULL) { *history = allocate_results(10); *history_count = 0; }\n397\t    if raw_load(history).is_null() {\n398\t        raw_store(history, allocate_results(10));\n399\t        raw_store(history_count, 0);\n400\t    }\n401\t\n402\t    if raw_load(history_count) < 10 {\n403\t        // Each of the three statements below re-reads `*history` and\n404\t        // `*history_count` because the C source spells the subscript out again\n405\t        // every time; that only matters if the caller aliases the two\n406\t        // out-parameters, and in that case this matches the C exactly.\n407\t\n408\t        // (*history)[*history_count].value = result;\n409\t        let entry = slot(raw_load(history), raw_load(history_count));\n410\t        raw_store(&raw mut (*entry).value, result);\n411\t\n412\t        // (*history)[*history_count].timestamp = get_computation_timestamp();\n413\t        let timestamp = get_computation_timestamp();\n414\t        let entry = slot(raw_load(history), raw_load(history_count));\n415\t        raw_store(&raw mut (*entry).timestamp, timestamp);\n416\t\n417\t        // (*